---
/**
 * Full-screen intro splash: the icon mark draws on first, then the
 * "inking" strokes draw on one at a time like a pen writing them,
 * "storeys" then pops in letter by letter, the whole mark holds, then
 * the icon flies from its splash position to exactly where its
 * counterpart already sits in the fixed Nav header (which has been
 * rendered underneath, hidden, this whole time) - Nav is icon-only, so
 * the wordmark just fades out rather than flying anywhere - while the
 * terracotta backdrop dissolves out from behind them. Reads as the icon
 * relocating into the navbar rather than two unrelated elements
 * swapping places. Shown once per browser session (sessionStorage-
 * gated) - repeat page loads within the same tab skip straight past it.
 *
 * The animation itself is pure CSS (`@keyframes` below), not JS. That's
 * a deliberate choice: an earlier version drove this with a GSAP
 * timeline, which meant a failed/slow GSAP import could leave this
 * full-viewport opaque panel stuck on screen forever with no way to
 * dismiss it - the worst possible failure mode for something in the
 * critical path of every page load. CSS animations start as soon as
 * the browser paints the element, with no JS dependency to fail. JS
 * here is only a *progressive enhancement* layer on top: it decides
 * whether to skip the animation at all (repeat visit, reduced-motion),
 * measures each "inking" stroke's path length (there's no CSS-only way
 * to do this - see the script below) and the icon/wordmark's fly-to-nav
 * distance (ditto - it depends on the Nav's actual rendered position,
 * which varies by viewport size), locks page scroll while it plays,
 * and removes the (by then harmless, invisible) DOM node afterward.
 *
 * The SVG markup below is inlined rather than referenced via <img
 * src="/wordmark-animated.svg">, because the per-stroke/per-letter
 * animations need to select individual <path>/<use> elements from
 * outside the SVG - an <img> is opaque to CSS, so this had to become a
 * duplicate-but-editable copy of that file's content rather than a
 * reference to it. Where each path came from: source is the studio's
 * Illustrator master (converted via `pdftocairo -svg`, since it's a
 * PDF-compatible file), which - unlike the flattened/traced kit export
 * originally used for `/wordmark.svg` - keeps "storeys" as real
 * per-letter glyph references and "inking" as separate un-fused
 * vector paths per pen-stroke (dot, i, n, k, i+n, dot, g - the second
 * "i"+"n" were written as one continuous stroke without lifting the
 * pen, unlike the first). That separability is *why* this animation is
 * possible at all: the flattened kit SVGs are each a single fused
 * compound path with no per-letter or per-stroke boundaries to animate
 * independently.
 *
 * The icon mark draws on too, reusing `/icon.svg`'s existing path (the
 * one already used in Nav.astro) rather than anything from the
 * Illustrator file - the icon in that file turned out to be an
 * embedded raster scan, not vector art, so there was nothing to trace
 * a stroke through. That source path has two subpaths - a small ring
 * (#icon-ring, scales open from a dot) and a closed "S" swirl "body".
 *
 * The body path itself is NOT a thin centerline, though - it's the
 * full outline of an already-thick filled ribbon (traces one edge all
 * the way around, then back along the other edge, to close the loop),
 * which made it a bad candidate for a dash-drawn "pen line" animation:
 * stroking that outline directly just traces a faint hairline around
 * both edges, and revealing arbitrary arc-length windows of it (an
 * earlier version of this file did exactly that) draws two DIFFERENT,
 * both-plausible-looking parts of the outline rather than two distinct
 * strokes with a clean start/meeting point - there's no such point on
 * a closed ribbon-outline loop; the split has to be chosen relative to
 * the ribbon's actual CENTERLINE, not its outline.
 *
 * That centerline (#icon-body-hook/-outer below) was extracted
 * offline, not hand-traced: densely sampled the outline into ~3000
 * boundary points, built a Voronoi diagram of them, kept only the
 * interior vertices/edges (an approximation of the medial axis), then
 * found the graph's diameter (the two mutually-farthest vertices via
 * two Dijkstra passes) - for a ribbon that's a stroke-expanded single
 * open curve with no branches, this reliably finds its two true
 * endpoints and the shortest path between them is the centerline
 * itself (verified: endpoint-to-endpoint distance came out to within
 * 6 units of exactly half the outline's total length, which is exactly
 * what a constant-width ribbon's centerline should measure). Split at
 * its most-visually-bottom point (which landed at 52% of the
 * centerline's arc length - close to the middle, unlike the outline's
 * own 17/83 split, because the centerline doesn't have the outline's
 * end-cap-driven asymmetry) into #icon-body-hook (the shorter piece,
 * starting at the small hook near the ring) and #icon-body-outer (the
 * longer piece, starting at the top-line's tip), each simplified and
 * refit as a smooth Catmull-Rom-to-bezier curve. Both draw on as thin
 * strokes from their own natural starting point down to that shared
 * bottom point, then #icon-body-fill (the original thick ribbon
 * outline, filled rather than stroked) fades in on top once both
 * finish - see the <style> block for the actual keyframes/formulas.
 */
import * as glyphs from "./preloader/glyphs";
import * as iconPaths from "./preloader/icon-paths";
import * as inkingStrokes from "./preloader/inking-strokes";
---

<div id="preloader" class="fixed inset-0 z-[100] flex items-center justify-center">
  <div id="preloader-backdrop" class="absolute inset-0 bg-terracotta"></div>
  <!-- `relative` here isn't decorative - it's load-bearing. Positioned
       elements always paint above non-positioned ones regardless of DOM
       order, so without this, the absolutely-positioned backdrop above
       would paint over this SVG even though it comes first in the
       markup - the drawing animation would run correctly but stay
       completely hidden behind a solid backdrop the whole time. -->
  <svg
    id="preloader-mark"
    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"
    viewBox="32 102 191 219"
    class="relative h-[34vh] w-auto max-w-[80vw] md:h-[38vh]"
    aria-label="Inking Storeys"
    role="img"
  >
    <defs>
      <g id="glyph-0-0">
        <path d={glyphs.s}/>
      </g>
      <g id="glyph-0-1">
        <path d={glyphs.t}/>
      </g>
      <g id="glyph-0-2">
        <path d={glyphs.o}/>
      </g>
      <g id="glyph-0-3">
        <path d={glyphs.r}/>
      </g>
      <g id="glyph-0-4">
        <path d={glyphs.e}/>
      </g>
      <g id="glyph-0-5">
        <path d={glyphs.y}/>
      </g>
    </defs>

    <!-- The icon mark, positioned above the wordmark to match the
         static full-lockup layout (see logo.svg/Footer.astro) - the
         inner translate/scale maps its own 0-395/0-699 viewBox into
         this shared canvas. That positioning lives on an *inner* group
         rather than on #logo-icon itself because a CSS `transform`
         (from logo-fly, see the <style> block) replaces an element's
         own transform attribute rather than combining with it - if the
         positioning were on #logo-icon, animating its CSS transform
         would discard this placement instead of flying from it.

         Split into a ring and a swirl "body" (see the top-of-file
         comment for the full story on how the body's centerline was
         extracted). #icon-body-fill is the original thick ribbon
         outline (filled, not stroked), invisible until the draw-on
         finishes. #icon-body-hook and #icon-body-outer are the two
         derived thin centerline strokes, each drawn on independently
         from their own natural start point down to the shared bottom
         meeting point - see icon-body-draw and the --draw-t comment
         further down for the dash-offset math. -->
    <g id="logo-icon">
      <g transform="translate(92.5,108) scale(0.177215)">
      <g transform="translate(0.000000,699.000000) scale(0.100000,-0.100000)">
        <path id="icon-ring" d={iconPaths.ring}/>
        <path id="icon-body-fill" fill="#ffffff" d={iconPaths.bodyFill}/>
        <path id="icon-body-hook" d={iconPaths.bodyHook}/>
        <path id="icon-body-outer" d={iconPaths.bodyOuter}/>
        <!-- "Pen tip" dots: ride #icon-body-hook/-outer's own growing
             end via CSS Motion Path (offset-path set to the exact same
             d as each stroke, offset-distance animated 0%->100% in
             lockstep with --draw-t on the strokes themselves - see the
             icon-body-dot-move comment in the <style> block for why
             this uses offset-distance directly instead of the
             --draw-t/calc() indirection everything else here needs).
             cx/cy are irrelevant once offset-path takes over
             positioning - 0,0 is just a neutral default. -->
        <circle id="icon-body-hook-dot" cx="0" cy="0" r="70"/>
        <circle id="icon-body-outer-dot" cx="0" cy="0" r="70"/>
      </g>
      </g>
    </g>

    <!-- Wraps both words so the "fly to nav" transform (see logo-fly
         below) moves "inking" and "storeys" together as one rigid
         unit, matching how they're already positioned relative to each
         other, rather than needing two more separately-computed
         transforms. -->
    <g id="logo-wordmark">
    <!-- Each path is one un-lifted pen-stroke, in writing order: dot of
         the 1st "i", its stem, "n", "k", the 2nd "i"+"n" (written as one
         stroke), that "i"'s dot, then "g". Drawn via stroke-dasharray/
         dashoffset (see ink-stroke-draw below), then snaps to filled -
         the standard "signature draw-on" technique. -->
    <g id="word-inking">
      <path id="ink-dot1" fill-rule="nonzero" d={inkingStrokes.dot1}/>
      <path id="ink-i1" fill-rule="nonzero" d={inkingStrokes.i1}/>
      <path id="ink-n1" fill-rule="nonzero" d={inkingStrokes.n1}/>
      <path id="ink-k" fill-rule="nonzero" d={inkingStrokes.k}/>
      <path id="ink-in2" fill-rule="nonzero" d={inkingStrokes.in2}/>
      <path id="ink-dot2" fill-rule="nonzero" d={inkingStrokes.dot2}/>
      <path id="ink-g" fill-rule="nonzero" d={inkingStrokes.g}/>
    </g>

    <!-- Real per-letter glyph refs (s-t-o-r-e-y-s), each popped in with
         its own staggered delay - see letter-pop-in below. -->
    <g id="word-storeys" fill="#ffffff">
      <use id="storeys-0" xlink:href="#glyph-0-0" x="87.700404" y="305.026895"/>
      <use id="storeys-1" xlink:href="#glyph-0-1" x="99.151912" y="305.026895"/>
      <use id="storeys-2" xlink:href="#glyph-0-2" x="109.27119" y="305.026895"/>
      <use id="storeys-3" xlink:href="#glyph-0-3" x="123.089531" y="305.026895"/>
      <use id="storeys-4" xlink:href="#glyph-0-4" x="132.953702" y="305.026895"/>
      <use id="storeys-5" xlink:href="#glyph-0-5" x="146.247655" y="305.026895"/>
      <use id="storeys-6" xlink:href="#glyph-0-0" x="158.492831" y="305.026895"/>
    </g>
    </g>
  </svg>
</div>

<style define:vars={{ bodyHookD: JSON.stringify(iconPaths.bodyHook), bodyOuterD: JSON.stringify(iconPaths.bodyOuter) }}>
  /* Every "draw the line on" animation in this file was silently
     snapping to its end state at the halfway point of its duration
     instead of drawing on smoothly - confirmed by sampling computed
     stroke-dashoffset over time in a real (Playwright-driven Chromium)
     browser, not just reasoning about it. Root cause: `stroke-dashoffset`
     keyframes whose VALUES are calc()/var() expressions referencing
     --stroke-length (e.g. `var(--stroke-length)` at 0% and
     `calc(var(--stroke-length) * 0.826)` at 100%) don't interpolate
     smoothly even when --stroke-length itself is registered via
     @property - confirmed with an isolated minimal repro outside this
     file. What DOES work (also confirmed with an isolated repro): give
     the animation its own dedicated, registered, non-inherited custom
     property that keyframes set to plain LITERAL numbers (no var()/
     calc() inside the keyframes themselves), then reference that
     property from a *static* (non-animated) calc() expression on the
     element to compute the actual stroke-dashoffset every frame. --draw-t
     below is that dedicated 0->1 progress variable; see its use on
     #word-inking path, #icon-body-hook, and #icon-body-outer further down. */
  @property --draw-t {
    syntax: "<number>";
    inherits: false;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  /* --stroke-length (set per-element by the script below, from
     getTotalLength()) also needs registering: cross-browser tested with
     Playwright's Chromium and Firefox builds, and Firefox would not
     evaluate a calc() that mixed this (unregistered) with the
     registered --draw-t above at all - the whole stroke-dashoffset
     formula silently fell back to 0 instead of erroring, so this was
     easy to miss without actually sampling computed styles in Firefox
     specifically. */
  @property --stroke-length {
    syntax: "<number>";
    inherits: false;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  /* The dot/ring: no stroke-draw at all, just a filled shape that
     scales up from near-zero at its own center. At small scale the
     hole is sub-pixel and it reads as a solid dot; growing it makes
     the ring open up organically, the way a pen-drawn dot fattens if
     you don't lift the nib. */
  #icon-ring {
    fill: #ffffff;
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: center;
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* The permanent thick-ribbon icon, invisible until the two thin
     centerline strokes below finish drawing on, then fades in on top
     of them (see icon-body-fill-in below) - the "signature draw-on,
     then snaps to filled" technique, same idea as ink-stroke-draw,
     just via opacity instead of a fill-color snap since this shape's
     stroke and fill outline aren't the same geometry (see the
     top-of-file comment for why: this path is a ribbon OUTLINE, not a
     stroke-friendly centerline). */
  #icon-body-fill {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  #icon-body-hook,
  #icon-body-outer {
    fill: none;
    stroke: #ffffff;
    /* Bigger than the wordmark strokes' stroke-width (0.6) because this
       path lives in `icon.svg`'s own much larger native coordinate
       space (0-395/0-699, vs. the wordmark's ~180-wide space) - it
       gets scaled back down by #logo-icon's transform along with
       everything else. */
    stroke-width: 35;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }
  /* --draw-t (see @property above) is what the animation actually
     touches (see icon-body-draw below); these compute the real
     stroke-dashoffset from it every frame, so the *animated* property
     (--draw-t) only ever carries a plain 0->1 number, never a
     var()/calc() expression, which is what makes it interpolate
     smoothly (see the @property comment above for why). Both strokes
     are now simple, single-direction, start-to-end reveals - no more
     forward/backward split-and-meet arithmetic, since #icon-body-hook
     and #icon-body-outer are already two separate paths, each running
     from its own natural start point down to the shared bottom point
     (see the top-of-file comment for how that split was derived). The
     trailing `* 1px` on both dasharray and dashoffset is load-bearing,
     not decorative: Chromium accepts a bare number for these
     properties, but Firefox requires the calc() to resolve to an
     explicit <length> - without a unit anywhere in the expression it
     silently computes to `none`/`0px` instead of erroring. Confirmed
     with an isolated repro across both engines before finding this. */
  #icon-body-hook {
    stroke-dasharray: calc(var(--stroke-length, 10666) * 1px);
    stroke-dashoffset: calc(var(--stroke-length, 10666) * (1 - var(--draw-t, 0)) * 1px);
  }
  #icon-body-outer {
    stroke-dasharray: calc(var(--stroke-length, 9776) * 1px);
    stroke-dashoffset: calc(var(--stroke-length, 9776) * (1 - var(--draw-t, 0)) * 1px);
  }

  /* "Pen tip" dots that ride each stroke's own growing end, via CSS
     Motion Path rather than the --draw-t/calc() approach everything
     else here uses - `offset-distance` is a normal, natively-
     animatable property (unlike stroke-dashoffset built from a
     var()/calc() expression), so it interpolates smoothly with a
     plain 0%->100% keyframe with no custom-property indirection
     needed - confirmed with an isolated repro in both Chromium and
     Firefox before wiring this up. `offset-path` is set to the exact
     same `d` as the matching stroke via `var(--bodyHookD)`/
     `var(--bodyOuterD)` (see `define:vars` on this <style> tag, and
     `src/components/preloader/icon-paths.ts`) rather than a second
     literal copy of that huge string, so the dot always sits precisely
     on the tip of the line currently being drawn and there's exactly
     one place each path's data lives. A CSS custom property can hold
     a quoted string and be substituted straight into `path()` -
     confirmed with an isolated repro in both engines before relying on
     it here, same as everything else non-obvious in this file - but
     `define:vars` itself doesn't add those quotes for you (it writes
     the raw value as-is), so the frontmatter passes
     `JSON.stringify(iconPaths.bodyHook)` rather than the bare string;
     without it `path()` silently computes to `none` instead of
     erroring, exactly like every other "this looks like it should
     just work" CSS gotcha logged in this file today. */
  #icon-body-hook-dot,
  #icon-body-outer-dot {
    fill: #ffffff;
    opacity: 0;
  }
  #icon-body-hook-dot {
    offset-path: path(var(--bodyHookD));
  }
  #icon-body-outer-dot {
    offset-path: path(var(--bodyOuterD));
  }

  #word-inking path {
    fill: none;
    stroke: #ffffff;
    stroke-width: 0.6;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    /* See the #icon-body-hook/-outer dasharray comment - same `* 1px`
       requirement for Firefox to actually apply the dash pattern. */
    stroke-dasharray: calc(var(--stroke-length, 1000) * 1px);
    /* See the --draw-t comment above #icon-body-hook - same reasoning
       (including the load-bearing `* 1px` for Firefox): --draw-t
       (animated by ink-stroke-draw below, 0->1 by 65% then held) is
       what's actually animated; this static formula turns that into
       the real dashoffset every frame. */
    stroke-dashoffset: calc(var(--stroke-length, 1000) * (1 - var(--draw-t, 0)) * 1px);
    opacity: 0;
  }

  #word-storeys use {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(4px);
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: center;
  }

  /*
   * `--fly-x`/`--fly-y`/`--fly-scale` are set by the script below, from
   * measuring where #logo-icon actually sits vs. its counterpart in
   * Nav.astro. `transform-origin: 0 0` (top-left, matching how
   * getBoundingClientRect reports position) keeps the translate/scale
   * math in the script simple: scaling anchors at the top-left corner,
   * so animating to `translate(dx, dy) scale(s)` - where dx/dy is just
   * the delta between the two rects' top-left corners - lands this
   * group's top-left exactly on the Nav element's top-left, no extra
   * centering correction needed.
   */
  #logo-icon {
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: 0 0;
  }

  @keyframes ink-stroke-draw {
    /*
     * Every property below is repeated at all three stops on purpose:
     * a CSS keyframe that omits a property doesn't mean "unchanged
     * since the last stop" - if a property is only ever declared at
     * one stop, its value is held constant for the *whole* animation,
     * not just from that point on. `stroke` only being declared at
     * `100%` (its intended end state) previously meant `stroke: none`
     * applied for the entire draw phase too, not just the end - so
     * nothing was ever visible until each stroke's very last instant,
     * which read as strokes "disappearing" right after being drawn.
     * --draw-t (not stroke-dashoffset directly - see the @property
     * comment near the top of this block) ramps 0->1 by 65%, then
     * holds at 1 through 100%; the actual stroke-dashoffset is computed
     * from it on #word-inking path above.
     */
    0% {
      opacity: 1;
      fill: none;
      stroke: #ffffff;
      --draw-t: 0;
    }
    65% {
      opacity: 1;
      fill: none;
      stroke: #ffffff;
      --draw-t: 1;
    }
    100% {
      opacity: 1;
      fill: #ffffff;
      stroke: #ffffff;
      --draw-t: 1;
    }
  }

  @keyframes icon-ring-expand {
    0% {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: scale(0.05);
    }
    100% {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: scale(1);
    }
  }

  /*
   * Drives #icon-body-hook and #icon-body-outer's shared --draw-t 0->1
   * (see the @property comment near the top of this block, and the
   * stroke-dashoffset formulas on those two selectors above - each
   * derives its own length from the same plain 0->1 number, which is
   * why one shared keyframe covers both). Both start fully undrawn and
   * finish fully drawn at the same instant, landing on the same
   * bottom point (see the top-of-file comment for how that point was
   * found), so the two strokes appear to travel from the icon's two
   * ends and meet at the bottom.
   */
  @keyframes icon-body-draw {
    0% {
      --draw-t: 0;
    }
    100% {
      --draw-t: 1;
    }
  }

  /* Moves the pen-tip dots along their own stroke (see the
     #icon-body-hook-dot/-outer-dot comment above) - offset-distance
     tracks the same 0->1 progress as --draw-t above (same duration/
     easing on the .run trigger keeps them in lockstep), fading out
     right at the end since the tip has nothing left to mark once its
     stroke is fully drawn. */
  @keyframes icon-body-dot-move {
    0% {
      opacity: 1;
      offset-distance: 0%;
    }
    95% {
      opacity: 1;
      offset-distance: 95%;
    }
    100% {
      opacity: 0;
      offset-distance: 100%;
    }
  }

  @keyframes icon-body-fill-in {
    to {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }

  @keyframes letter-pop-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(4px);
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: translateY(0);
    }
  }

  @keyframes logo-fly {
    from {
      transform: translate(0, 0) scale(1);
    }
    to {
      transform: translate(var(--fly-x, 0px), var(--fly-y, 0px)) scale(var(--fly-scale, 1));
    }
  }

  @keyframes wordmark-fade-out {
    from {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    to {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  @keyframes preloader-backdrop-out {
    from {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    to {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  /*
   * Timeline (only runs once the JS below adds the `.run` class - see
   * "why gate this behind JS?" note in the script). The icon's own
   * draw-on (ring + body) now takes 4.0s - up from an original 0.85s,
   * same 35/65 split between the two stages - and everything after it
   * keeps its original durations from the 4.8s-total version, just
   * pushed back by the 3.15s difference. Total is now 7.95s:
   *   0.00s - 2.60s the "S" body's two thin centerline strokes draw on
   *                 from their own ends at once, meeting at the bottom
   *                 (see icon-body-draw above), each with a "pen tip"
   *                 dot riding its growing end (icon-body-dot-move) and
   *                 fading out right as it lands; then the permanent
   *                 thick-ribbon icon fades in on top of them
   *                 (icon-body-fill-in, 2.60s - 2.80s)
   *   2.60s - 4.00s the ring pops open from a dot
   *   4.00s - 5.95s the 7 "inking" strokes draw in one at a time, 0.24s
   *                 apart (last two - the 2nd i's dot and "g" - given a
   *                 bit more room since they read as separate beats)
   *   5.85s - 6.75s "storeys"'s 7 letters pop in, 0.1s apart, starting
   *                 just before the last ink stroke finishes so the two
   *                 words flow together rather than pausing between them
   *   6.75s - 7.15s hold, fully written
   *   7.15s - 7.90s icon flies from its splash position to exactly
   *                 where it sits in Nav.astro (see logo-fly); the
   *                 wordmark has nowhere to fly to (Nav is icon-only),
   *                 so it fades out instead (wordmark-fade-out, 7.15s -
   *                 7.65s) rather than hanging in place
   *   7.25s - 7.95s backdrop dissolves (starts just after the fly
   *                 begins, so the page doesn't show through before the
   *                 icon starts moving; ends just after the fly lands,
   *                 so nothing's visible behind the mark mid-flight)
   */
  #preloader.run #icon-body-hook,
  #preloader.run #icon-body-outer {
    animation: icon-body-draw 2.6s ease-out 0s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #icon-body-hook-dot,
  #preloader.run #icon-body-outer-dot {
    animation: icon-body-dot-move 2.6s ease-out 0s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #icon-body-fill {
    animation: icon-body-fill-in 0.2s ease-out 2.6s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #icon-ring {
    animation: icon-ring-expand 1.4s ease-out 2.6s forwards;
  }

  #preloader.run #word-inking path:nth-child(1) {
    animation: ink-stroke-draw 0.4s ease-out 4.0s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-inking path:nth-child(2) {
    animation: ink-stroke-draw 0.4s ease-out 4.24s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-inking path:nth-child(3) {
    animation: ink-stroke-draw 0.4s ease-out 4.48s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-inking path:nth-child(4) {
    animation: ink-stroke-draw 0.45s ease-out 4.72s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-inking path:nth-child(5) {
    animation: ink-stroke-draw 0.5s ease-out 5.0s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-inking path:nth-child(6) {
    animation: ink-stroke-draw 0.35s ease-out 5.35s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-inking path:nth-child(7) {
    animation: ink-stroke-draw 0.4s ease-out 5.55s forwards;
  }

  #preloader.run #word-storeys use:nth-child(1) {
    animation: letter-pop-in 0.3s ease-out 5.85s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-storeys use:nth-child(2) {
    animation: letter-pop-in 0.3s ease-out 5.95s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-storeys use:nth-child(3) {
    animation: letter-pop-in 0.3s ease-out 6.05s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-storeys use:nth-child(4) {
    animation: letter-pop-in 0.3s ease-out 6.15s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-storeys use:nth-child(5) {
    animation: letter-pop-in 0.3s ease-out 6.25s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-storeys use:nth-child(6) {
    animation: letter-pop-in 0.3s ease-out 6.35s forwards;
  }
  #preloader.run #word-storeys use:nth-child(7) {
    animation: letter-pop-in 0.3s ease-out 6.45s forwards;
  }

  #preloader.run #logo-icon {
    animation: logo-fly 0.75s cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1) 7.15s forwards;
  }
  /* Nav no longer shows the wordmark (icon-only now), so there's
     nowhere for this half of the mark to fly to - it fades out instead
     of hanging motionless in the middle of the screen while the icon
     flies off and the backdrop dissolves around it. */
  #preloader.run #logo-wordmark {
    animation: wordmark-fade-out 0.5s ease-in 7.15s forwards;
  }

  #preloader.run #preloader-backdrop {
    animation: preloader-backdrop-out 0.7s ease-in 7.25s forwards;
  }
</style>

<script>
  function runPreloader() {
    const el = document.getElementById("preloader");
    if (!el) return;

    const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;

    if (sessionStorage.getItem("inking-storeys-preloaded") || reduceMotion) {
      // Already shown this session, or the visitor asked for reduced
      // motion - skip straight to removing it, no animation.
      el.remove();
      return;
    }

    sessionStorage.setItem("inking-storeys-preloaded", "1");
    document.documentElement.style.overflow = "hidden";

    // The icon body and each "inking" stroke draw on via stroke-dasharray/
    // dashoffset, which needs each path's actual rendered length -
    // there's no CSS-only way to get this (unlike a fixed-length font
    // glyph), so it's measured here per-path and exposed to the static
    // calc() formulas (see the --draw-t comment near the top of the
    // <style> block) as a custom property.
    el.querySelectorAll<SVGPathElement>("#icon-body-hook, #icon-body-outer, #word-inking path").forEach((path) => {
      path.style.setProperty("--stroke-length", String(path.getTotalLength()));
    });

    // Sets up the "fly to nav" transform (logo-fly, above): measures
    // where the source group currently sits vs. where its counterpart
    // already sits in Nav.astro (rendered underneath the opaque
    // backdrop this whole time, at its real final position/size for
    // the current viewport - nothing about Nav changes once the page
    // has loaded, so there's no need to re-measure right before the fly
    // actually starts). `transform-origin: 0 0` on #logo-icon (see the
    // <style> block) means this only needs the top-left corners' delta
    // and a single width-based scale ratio, not any centering math. Nav
    // is icon-only now, so this is only called for #logo-icon -
    // #logo-wordmark fades out instead (wordmark-fade-out, above).
    function setFlyTarget(source: Element | null, targetId: string) {
      const target = document.getElementById(targetId);
      if (!source || !target) return;
      const from = source.getBoundingClientRect();
      const to = target.getBoundingClientRect();
      const scale = to.width / from.width;
      (source as SVGElement).style.setProperty("--fly-x", `${to.left - from.left}px`);
      (source as SVGElement).style.setProperty("--fly-y", `${to.top - from.top}px`);
      (source as SVGElement).style.setProperty("--fly-scale", String(scale));
    }

    setFlyTarget(el.querySelector("#logo-icon"), "nav-icon");

    let done = false;
    const finish = () => {
      if (done) return;
      done = true;
      document.documentElement.style.overflow = "";
      el.remove();
    };

    el.addEventListener("animationend", (event) => {
      // #preloader has multiple animations running on descendants (see
      // the <style> block); only react to the backdrop's fade-out,
      // which is timed to be the last one to finish (see the timeline
      // comment above) and the one that actually needs cleanup.
      if (event.animationName === "preloader-backdrop-out") finish();
    });

    // Safety net: guarantee cleanup even if the animation is interrupted
    // (backgrounded tab, dropped frames) and its end event never fires.
    setTimeout(finish, 8750);

    // Why gate the CSS animation behind adding this class instead of
    // letting it run unconditionally the moment the element paints?
    // Two reasons: (1) it lets the sessionStorage/reduced-motion checks
    // above skip the animation entirely rather than racing to remove
    // the element mid-animation, and (2) on client-side navigations
    // (Astro's View Transitions router), a fresh #preloader element
    // mounts on every route change - without this gate it would replay
    // the intro animation on every internal link click, not just the
    // first visit.
    el.classList.add("run");
  }

  runPreloader();
</script>
